{"name":"Big Sur","hex":"#b3cadc","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Big Sur","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A rugged and mountainous section of the Central Coast of California."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Big_Sur"}],"entries":[{"title":"Big Sur","blocks":[{"text":"Big Sur is a rugged and mountainous section of the Central Coast of the U.S. state of California, between Carmel Highlands and San Simeon, where the Santa Lucia Mountains rise abruptly from the Pacific Ocean. It is frequently praised for its dramatic scenery. Big Sur has been called the \"longest and most scenic stretch of undeveloped coastline in the contiguous United States\", a sublime \"national treasure that demands extraordinary procedures to protect it from development\", and \"one of the most beautiful coastlines anywhere in the world, an isolated stretch of road, mythic in reputation\". The views, redwood forests, hiking, beaches, and other recreational opportunities have made Big Sur a popular destination for visitors from across the world. With 4.5 to 7 million visitors annually, it is among the top tourist destinations in the United States, comparable to Yosemite National Park, but with considerably fewer services, and less parking, roads, and related infrastructure."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Central_Californian_Coastline%2C_Big_Sur_-_May_2013.jpg/330px-Central_Californian_Coastline%2C_Big_Sur_-_May_2013.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Big_Sur","wikidata":"Q859413"},{"title":"Big Sur (novel)","blocks":[{"text":"Big Sur is a 1962 novel by Jack Kerouac, written in the fall of 1961 over a ten-day period, with Kerouac typewriting onto a teletype roll. It recounts the events surrounding Kerouac's three brief sojourns to a cabin in Bixby Canyon, Big Sur, California, owned by Kerouac's friend and Beat poet Lawrence Ferlinghetti; at the same time dealing with his increased drinking and declining mental health. It is Kerouac’s first novel to be fully written following his success in the late 1950s, and thus departs from his previous fictionalized autobiographical series in that the character Duluoz is shown as a popular, published author; most of Kerouac's previous novels instead portray him as a bohemian traveller."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/c/ce/BigSur.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Big_Sur_(novel)","wikidata":"Q2571011"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Big_Sur_and_Los_Padres_National_Forest.png/500px-Big_Sur_and_Los_Padres_National_Forest.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Big_Sur_and_Los_Padres_National_Forest.png/1280px-Big_Sur_and_Los_Padres_National_Forest.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Big_Sur_and_Los_Padres_National_Forest.png/500px-Big_Sur_and_Los_Padres_National_Forest.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" Approximate boundaries of the Big Sur region","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABig_Sur_and_Los_Padres_National_Forest.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Gorda%2C_California_2013-12-22_%28a%29.jpg/500px-Gorda%2C_California_2013-12-22_%28a%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Gorda%2C_California_2013-12-22_%28a%29.jpg/1280px-Gorda%2C_California_2013-12-22_%28a%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Gorda%2